/* ---------- Theory for every lesson ----------
   learn[] = the explanation (may contain <b>), practice[] = how to practise, watch[] = common mistakes.
   Shown under "Learn more" in the lesson intro, which can be reopened at any time. */
const THEORY = {
  b1:{learn:["A piano has 88 keys, but only <b>12 different notes</b> that repeat over and over: 7 white keys named with the letters <b>A to G</b>, and 5 black keys in between. Because the pattern repeats, the black keys are your map: you can always find your place by looking at them.",
             "<b>Middle C</b> is the C closest to the middle of the keyboard, usually just below the piano's brand name. In sheet music it sits on a short line of its own between the treble clef (right hand) and the bass clef (left hand), which is why both hands start from it.",
             "Every C on the piano is the white key directly to the <b>left of a group of two black keys</b>."],
      practice:["Sit opposite middle C, with your elbows slightly in front of your body and your forearms level with the keys.","Play with the side of your thumb's tip, and let the key come all the way up before you play it again.","Say \"C\" out loud each time you play it. Naming notes while you play builds the link between a name and its place."],
      watch:["Hitting the key next to C (B or D). Find the two black keys first, then the white key on their left.","Keeping the key down. In <i>Wait for me</i> the next note waits for you, so release and get ready."]},
  b2:{learn:["Pianists number the fingers of <b>both hands</b> the same way: thumb = <b>1</b>, index = <b>2</b>, middle = <b>3</b>, ring = <b>4</b>, pinky = <b>5</b>. Finger numbers above notes in sheet music (and on the falling notes here) tell you which finger to use.",
             "Putting one finger on each of five neighbouring white keys is a <b>five-finger position</b>. With your thumb on C you're in <b>C position</b>: C D E F G. Many first songs fit entirely in it, so you can play them without looking at your hands.",
             "A <b>curved hand</b> is the strongest: the fingertips press the keys and the wrist stays level, neither drooping nor raised."],
      practice:["Before you start, rest all five fingers on C D E F G and look at them once. Then keep your eyes on the screen.","Play slowly and evenly. Speed comes by itself once each finger knows its key.","Imagine holding a small ball in your palm to keep the curve."],
      watch:["Flat fingers, or the last joint of finger 4 or 5 collapsing. Keep the fingertips round.","Your thumb sliding off C. It's the anchor of the position."]},
  b3:{learn:["Moving to the very next key is a <b>step</b>. Skipping one key, like C to E, is a <b>skip</b>. In music theory a skip is called a <b>third</b>, because you count the letter names: C (1), D (2), E (3).",
             "Melodies are built from steps, skips and bigger leaps. Recognising them is the start of reading music: in sheet music a step moves from a line to the next space, and a skip jumps from line to line or from space to space."],
      practice:["Keep all five fingers resting on their keys, even the ones that aren't playing.","Say \"step\" or \"skip\" for each move while you play."],
      watch:["Moving your whole hand to reach a key. In this lesson every note is already under a finger."]},
  b4:{learn:["The left hand uses the same numbers as the right (thumb 1, pinky 5), but it's a mirror image, so the <b>pinky plays the lowest note</b>. In C position the left hand plays C (5) D (4) E (3) F (2) G (1), one octave below middle C.",
             "The left hand usually plays the <b>lower part</b> of the music: bass notes and chords that support the melody. In sheet music it reads the <b>bass clef</b>. For most people it's the weaker hand at first, and short daily practice catches it up quickly.",
             "In Keys, left-hand notes are <b style=\"color:var(--left)\">orange</b> and right-hand notes are <b style=\"color:var(--accent)\">blue</b>."],
      practice:["Play the same five notes you played with the right hand and compare: same notes, opposite fingers.","Practise slowly with the left hand alone."],
      watch:["Using right-hand fingering (thumb on C). Your left pinky goes on C.","Letting the pinky go flat. Keep it slightly curved and play on its tip."]},
  b5:{learn:["Black keys raise or lower the white keys next to them by a <b>half step</b> (also called a semitone), the smallest distance on the piano. A <b>sharp (♯)</b> raises a note: C♯ is the black key just right of C. A <b>flat (♭)</b> lowers a note: D♭ is the black key just left of D.",
             "So C♯ and D♭ are the <b>same key with two names</b>. Which name is used depends on the key the music is in.",
             "There's <b>no black key between E and F, or between B and C</b>. Those white keys are already a half step apart, and that's what splits the black keys into groups of two and three."],
      practice:["Play the group of two with fingers 2 and 3, and the group of three with 2, 3 and 4.","Play black keys near their front edge with curved fingers."],
      watch:["Looking for a black key between E and F, or between B and C. There isn't one."]},
  b6:{learn:["The <b>beat</b> (or pulse) is the steady heartbeat of music, the thing you tap your foot to. Notes can be longer or shorter, but the beat keeps going at an even speed underneath them.",
             "<b>Tempo</b> is how fast the beat goes, measured in <b>beats per minute (BPM)</b>. At 60 BPM there's one beat every second. Italian words describe tempo in sheet music: <i>Andante</i> (walking pace), <i>Moderato</i> (moderate), <i>Allegro</i> (fast).",
             "In <i>Play along</i> the music doesn't wait, so playing <b>on time</b> matters as much as playing the right key. The metronome clicks every beat, with an accent on beat 1 of each bar."],
      practice:["Count out loud with the clicks before you play: \"1 2 3 4\".","If it's too fast, lower the speed and work up to 100%. The speed trainer does this for you.","Hold long notes until the next note arrives."],
      watch:["Rushing, which means playing ahead of the click. Wait for the line.","Stopping the count while a note is held. Keep counting through long notes."]},
  b7:{learn:["A <b>scale</b> is a set of notes going up or down in order. The <b>C major scale</b> uses only white keys: C D E F G A B C. The eighth note has the same name as the first, one octave higher.",
             "Every major scale follows the same pattern of <b>whole steps (W)</b> and <b>half steps (H)</b>: <b>W W H W W W H</b>. In C major the half steps fall between E–F and B–C, exactly where there's no black key.",
             "A scale has eight notes and you have five fingers, so pianists <b>pass the thumb under</b> the hand going up and <b>cross a finger over the thumb</b> coming down. The standard right-hand fingering is 1 2 3, 1 2 3 4 5."],
      practice:["Start moving your thumb towards F while finger 3 plays E, so it arrives in time.","Keep the wrist level and let the hand glide sideways, without your elbow sticking out.","Play the scale slowly and evenly every day. It's the best warm-up there is."],
      watch:["A bump in the rhythm at the thumb crossing. Aim for one smooth, even line.","Lifting the whole hand to reach F instead of passing the thumb under."]},
  b8:{learn:["Most simple melodies move mainly by step. <i>Mary Had a Little Lamb</i> uses only C D E and G, all in C position, so you can concentrate on rhythm and evenness.",
             "Songs are made of <b>phrases</b>, musical sentences that often repeat. This song has four phrases, and the first and third are almost identical. Spotting repeats makes any song much quicker to learn."],
      practice:["Sing or hum the melody while you play.","Find the phrase that repeats and play it twice in a row."],
      watch:["Repeated notes (E E E): lift the finger slightly between them so the key can come back up and sound again."]},
  b9:{learn:["The 12-key pattern of the keyboard repeats about seven times. Each repeat is an <b>octave</b>, the distance from one note to the next note with the same name. Notes an octave apart sound like the same note, higher or lower, because the higher one vibrates exactly <b>twice as fast</b>.",
             "Octaves are numbered so every key has a unique name: middle C is <b>C4</b>, the C below is C3 and the one above is C5. On an 88-key piano the lowest key is A0 and the highest is C8.",
             "Once you know where all the Cs are, you have a map of the whole keyboard."],
      practice:["Find each C by its group of two black keys rather than by counting white keys.","Say the octave number as you play: C2, C3, C4…"],
      watch:["Mixing up the groups: Cs sit by the groups of <b>two</b> black keys, Fs by the groups of <b>three</b>."]},
  b10:{learn:["Not every piece fits in C position, so pianists move the hand to new five-finger positions. In <b>G position</b> the right thumb goes on G: G A B C D.",
              "Moving the hand between phrases is a <b>position shift</b>. Good players shift during a rest or a long note, and land with all five fingers ready."],
       practice:["Shift during the gap, glance at where your thumb lands, then look back at the screen.","Practise just the shift a few times: C position to G position and back."],
       watch:["Shifting late, so the first note in the new position comes in late."]},
  r1:{learn:["Note lengths are measured in beats. A <b>quarter note</b> lasts 1 beat, a <b>half note</b> 2 beats and a <b>whole note</b> 4 beats. Each is twice as long as the one before.",
             "Music is grouped into <b>bars</b> (or measures), most often of 4 beats. That's the <b>time signature 4/4</b>: the top number says how many beats are in a bar, the bottom number which note gets one beat (4 = a quarter note).",
             "In Keys, the length of a falling note shows its duration: a half note is twice as tall as a quarter note."],
      practice:["Clap the rhythm while counting before you play it.","Release a half note exactly when the next note is due, not earlier."],
      watch:["Cutting half notes short. Hold them through beat 2."]},
  r2:{learn:["A <b>whole note</b> lasts 4 beats, a whole bar of 4/4.",
             "<b>Rests</b> are silences with exact lengths, just like notes: a quarter rest lasts 1 beat, a half rest 2 and a whole rest a full bar. Silence is part of the music, so count rests as carefully as notes."],
      practice:["Count through the rests, maybe a little more quietly, so they keep their length.","Lift your finger off the key right at the start of a rest."],
      watch:["Starting the note after a rest too early because the gap feels long."]},
  r3:{learn:["An <b>eighth note</b> lasts half a beat, so two fit in one beat. Count them \"1 <b>and</b> 2 <b>and</b> 3 <b>and</b> 4 <b>and</b>\". The numbers land on the click, and each \"and\" lands exactly halfway between.",
             "<b>Sixteenth notes</b> split the beat into four: \"1 e and a\". Every smaller note value is half of the one before."],
      practice:["Say \"1-and\" along with the metronome before you play.","Keep the eighth notes even, with both halves of the beat the same length."],
      watch:["Uneven, galloping eighths. Listen for an even \"ta-ta\"."]},
  r4:{learn:["A <b>dot</b> after a note adds half of its length again. A dotted half note = 2 + 1 = <b>3 beats</b>. A dotted quarter = 1 + ½ = <b>1½ beats</b>.",
             "A dotted quarter is usually followed by an eighth note, together filling two beats: \"long – short\", counted \"1 (and) 2-and\". This rhythm gives a melody its lilt, like the end of each phrase of <i>Ode to Joy</i>."],
      practice:["Count in eighths: the dotted quarter lasts three eighths and the eighth note one."],
      watch:["Turning the dotted rhythm into two equal notes. The first note is three times as long as the second."]},
  r5:{learn:["The time signature <b>3/4</b> means three quarter-note beats per bar, counted \"<b>1</b> 2 3\". Beat 1 is strong and beats 2 and 3 are lighter, which gives the swaying \"OOM-pah-pah\" of a waltz, a minuet or <i>Happy Birthday</i>.",
             "Other common time signatures are <b>2/4</b> (marches: \"1 2\"), <b>4/4</b> (most pop music) and <b>6/8</b>, six eighth notes per bar felt as two big beats, like <i>Row, Row, Row Your Boat</i>."],
      practice:["Lean a little on beat 1 of every bar.","The classic waltz accompaniment: bass note on beat 1, chord on beats 2 and 3."],
      watch:["Adding a fourth beat out of 4/4 habit."]},
  r6:{learn:["Normally the strong beats (1 and 3 in 4/4) get the important notes. <b>Syncopation</b> shifts the emphasis onto weak beats or <b>between the beats</b>, on the \"and\". It creates energy and surprise, and you'll find it everywhere in ragtime, jazz, funk, Latin and pop music.",
             "A note that starts on an \"and\" and is held across the next beat makes the rhythm feel pushed forward."],
      practice:["Tap your foot on every beat and keep it going whatever your hands do. Your foot is the clock.","Count with the off-beats louder: \"1 AND 2 AND\"."],
      watch:["Moving the syncopated note onto the beat because that feels safer."]},
  r7:{learn:["Playing a different rhythm in each hand is called <b>hand independence</b>. A common place to start is a steady left hand, one note per beat, under a varied right-hand melody.",
             "The steady part is your timekeeper: if it stays even, the busier part has something solid to fit around."],
      practice:["Play the left hand alone until it's automatic, then add the right hand.","Practise slowly with the metronome. Speed comes later."],
      watch:["The left hand copying the right hand's rhythm. Keep it even."]},
  c1:{learn:["A <b>chord</b> is three or more notes played together. The basic chord, a <b>triad</b>, has three: the <b>root</b> (the note it's named after), the <b>third</b> (two letter names up) and the <b>fifth</b> (four letter names up). C major = C (root), E (third), G (fifth).",
             "On the white keys it looks like play – skip – play – skip – play. The distances decide its sound: C to E is 4 half steps (a <b>major third</b>) and E to G is 3 (a <b>minor third</b>). Major chords sound bright and settled.",
             "Chords make the <b>harmony</b>, the colour underneath a melody."],
      practice:["Place fingers 1, 3 and 5 on C, E and G first, then press all three at exactly the same moment.","Let your hand drop into the keys from the wrist rather than pushing with each finger."],
      watch:["Rolled chords, where one note sounds before the others. The app waits until all three are down together.","Finger 3 collapsing. Keep the hand arched."]},
  c2:{learn:["Any note can be the root of a chord: F A C is <b>F major</b> and G B D is <b>G major</b>. They're major because their thirds are the same size as C major's.",
             "C, F and G are the three main chords of C major, built on the 1st, 4th and 5th notes of the scale. With just these three chords you can accompany hundreds of songs."],
      practice:["Freeze your hand shape and move the whole hand from chord to chord.","Look at where the root lands, then trust the shape for the other two notes."],
      watch:["The shape changing as you move. Keep exactly one white key skipped between each finger."]},
  c3:{learn:["A <b>chord progression</b> is a sequence of chords. Musicians name chords by their place in the scale using <b>Roman numerals</b>: in C major, C = <b>I</b>, F = <b>IV</b> and G = <b>V</b>.",
             "Each chord has a job. <b>I</b> (the tonic) feels like home. <b>IV</b> (the subdominant) moves away. <b>V</b> (the dominant) builds tension that wants to resolve to I. That pull from V back to I is the most important movement in Western harmony.",
             "Because numerals describe roles rather than notes, I – IV – V – I works in every key: in G major it's G – C – D – G."],
      practice:["Listen for the \"coming home\" feeling when G goes back to C.","Count four beats per chord so the changes land in time."],
      watch:["Pausing before every chord change. Prepare the next shape while you count the last beat."]},
  c4:{learn:["A <b>minor chord</b> has the same root and fifth as a major chord, but its third is <b>one half step lower</b>. The bottom gap becomes a minor third (3 half steps) and the top one a major third (4). That single half step turns bright into dark.",
             "In every major key, the chords on the 2nd, 3rd and 6th notes are minor. In C major they're <b>D minor, E minor and A minor</b>, all on white keys.",
             "Chord symbols: \"C\" on its own means C major; \"Cm\" means C minor."],
      practice:["Play C, then Cm, then C again, and listen to the change.","Hum the middle note as it moves down. That's the note that changes the mood."],
      watch:["Moving the wrong note. Only the middle note (the third) moves."]},
  c5:{learn:["A triad's notes can be stacked in any order. With the root at the bottom it's in <b>root position</b> (C E G). With the third at the bottom it's in <b>first inversion</b> (E G C), and with the fifth at the bottom it's in <b>second inversion</b> (G C E).",
             "It's still the same chord, because it has the same three notes. Inversions let you change chords smoothly: notes that two chords share stay where they are, and the others move by a step. This is called <b>voice leading</b>.",
             "C (C E G) to F (C F A): C stays put, E steps up to F and G steps up to A."],
      practice:["Before each change, find the finger that can stay put.","Move the other fingers by the smallest step possible."],
      watch:["Jumping the whole hand back to root position out of habit."]},
  c6:{learn:["In Roman numerals, C – G – Am – F is <b>I – V – vi – IV</b>. The lowercase vi shows that A minor is a minor chord. It's been nicknamed \"the four-chord song\" because so many hits use it: <i>Let It Be</i>, <i>Someone Like You</i>, <i>With or Without You</i> and many more.",
             "Start the same four chords on Am (vi – IV – I – V) and the mood turns more melancholy, as in <i>Zombie</i>. Same chords, different starting point, different feeling.",
             "<i>Practice → Pop chords</i> has more progressions to play in different styles."],
      practice:["Learn the four shapes as one connected movement and repeat it in a loop.","Once it's comfortable, try it in the Pop chords drill with the pop rhythm."],
      watch:["Losing the beat at the Am → F change, the trickiest of the four."]},
  c7:{learn:["Playing the notes of a chord one after another is an <b>arpeggio</b> (from the Italian <i>arpa</i>, harp). It spreads the harmony out in time and gives the music movement.",
             "Accompaniments are full of broken-chord patterns. The <b>Alberti bass</b> goes bottom – top – middle – top, and the famous opening of the <i>Moonlight Sonata</i> is a slow arpeggio pattern."],
      practice:["Keep the hand in the chord shape. Only the fingers move.","Aim for even notes, like a harp."],
      watch:["Rushing at the top of the arpeggio."]},
  c8:{learn:["In most piano music the <b>left hand plays the harmony</b> (chords or bass notes) while the right hand plays the melody. Left-hand chords usually sit in the octave below middle C, where they sound full without getting muddy.",
             "Shapes that share notes (C: C E G, F: C F A, G: B D G) keep the left hand in one place, just like right-hand inversions."],
      practice:["Learn the left-hand shapes on their own before adding a melody.","Play the left-hand chords a little softer than the melody so the tune stands out."],
      watch:["Chords placed too low. Below about C3 they start to sound muddy."]},
  c9:{learn:["A <b>seventh chord</b> adds a fourth note, a seventh above the root. G7 = G B D F. Because it's built on the 5th note of the scale it's called the <b>dominant seventh</b>, written V7.",
             "Inside G7, B and F are a <b>tritone</b> apart (6 half steps), the most unstable sound in music. It resolves naturally: B moves up to C and F moves down to E, landing on C major. That's why V7 → I sounds so final.",
             "A practical shape is B F G (fingers 1-4-5), which moves smoothly to C E G."],
      practice:["Play G7 → C slowly and listen to the tension release.","Notice the two moving notes: B → C (up a half step) and F → E (down a half step)."],
      watch:["Playing F♯ instead of F. G7 uses F natural."]},
  s1:{learn:["Both hands can use the same five-finger position in different octaves. In G position the right thumb goes on the G above middle C and the left pinky on the G below.",
             "A new position often means a new <b>key centre</b>, the note that feels like home. Pieces in G major feel settled when they land on G."],
      practice:["Before you start, check both hands: right thumb on a G, left pinky on the G below."],
      watch:["The left hand drifting back to its familiar C position."]},
  s2:{learn:["A five-finger position should sound like the first five notes of a major scale: <b>W W H W</b>. From D, white keys alone would give D E F G A, with the half step in the wrong place.",
             "Raising F to <b>F♯</b> fixes it: D E F♯ G A follows the major pattern. That's why keys need sharps and flats: to keep the same pattern whatever note you start on."],
      practice:["Rest finger 3 on F♯ before you begin. It sits further into the keyboard, so move the hand slightly forward."],
      watch:["Playing F instead of F♯ out of habit."]},
  s3:{learn:["The major pattern W W H W W W H from G gives G A B C D E <b>F♯</b> G. F has to be raised to F♯ so the last step is a half step.",
             "That one sharp is G major's <b>key signature</b>. In sheet music a single ♯ on the F line at the start of every line means every F is played as F♯.",
             "Each key has its own key signature. Adding one sharp at a time gives G (1♯), D (2♯), A (3♯) and so on, travelling round the <b>circle of fifths</b>."],
      practice:["Say \"sharp\" when you reach F♯, the first few times.","Use the same fingering as C major: 1 2 3, thumb under, 1 2 3 4 5."],
      watch:["Forgetting the F♯ on the way down."]},
  s4:{learn:["The major pattern from F gives F G A <b>B♭</b> C D E F. The fourth note is lowered to B♭ to make the half step between A and B♭.",
             "F major's key signature is one flat (B♭). Flat keys go the other way round the circle of fifths: F (1♭), B♭ (2♭), E♭ (3♭)…",
             "The fingering changes so the thumb never plays a black key: 1 2 3 4 (finger 4 on B♭), thumb under to C, then 1 2 3 4."],
      practice:["Play just F G A B♭ C a few times to get used to finger 4 on the black key."],
      watch:["Passing the thumb after A, which is the C major habit. In F major it comes after B♭."]},
  s5:{learn:["The <b>natural minor scale</b> has the pattern W H W W H W W. From A it uses only white keys: A B C D E F G A.",
             "A minor and C major share the same notes, which makes them <b>relative keys</b>. Every major key has a relative minor that starts on its 6th note.",
             "Many minor pieces raise the 7th note (G → G♯ in A minor) to pull more strongly back home. That's the <b>harmonic minor</b>, covered in the Music theory course."],
      practice:["Play C major, then A minor, and compare the moods.","Fingering: 1 2 3, thumb under, 1 2 3 4 5, just like C major."],
      watch:["Starting on C out of habit. A minor starts and ends on A."]},
  s6:{learn:["The left hand plays scales in mirror image. Going up: 5 4 3 2 1, then <b>finger 3 crosses over</b> the thumb to A, then 2 1. Coming down, the thumb passes under after finger 3.",
             "Scale fingerings are designed so the thumb avoids black keys and the crossings happen at natural points."],
      practice:["Practise just the crossing, G (1) to A (3) and back, until it's smooth."],
      watch:["Twisting the wrist at the crossing. Keep it level and let finger 3 reach over."]},
  s7:{learn:["With both hands together the fingerings differ, so the crossings happen at <b>different moments</b>. Going up from C, the right thumb passes under to F, while the left hand's finger 3 crosses over to A.",
             "It's a classic coordination exercise: each hand needs to be secure on its own before you combine them."],
      practice:["Go very slowly, saying the finger numbers of both hands.","Practise the two crossing points on their own until they feel natural."],
      watch:["One hand copying the other's crossing. Learn each hand separately first."]},
  h1:{learn:["Two-hand playing starts with <b>alternation</b>: one hand plays while the other rests and gets ready. Your brain switches between hands without having to run both at once.",
             "In Keys, the Right / Left setting lets you practise one hand while the app plays the other."],
      practice:["Keep the resting hand in position, hovering over its keys."],
      watch:["The resting hand wandering away from its position."]},
  h2:{learn:["<b>Parallel motion</b> means both hands move in the same direction. Playing the same notes an octave apart makes a melody sound fuller and stronger.",
             "Because the hands are mirror images, the same notes need different fingers: right hand 1 2 3 4 5, left hand 5 4 3 2 1."],
      practice:["Think in note names rather than finger numbers. Both hands share them."],
      watch:["Using the same finger numbers in both hands. They're mirrored."]},
  h3:{learn:["<b>Contrary motion</b> means the hands move in opposite directions, one up and one down. Here both hands use the same finger numbers, which feels natural because the hands are mirror images.",
             "Composers use contrary motion to open the music out or draw it together, and it's one of the best coordination exercises there is."],
      practice:["Start with both thumbs near middle C and move outwards together."],
      watch:["One hand following the other's direction."]},
  h4:{learn:["A very common texture: a <b>melody in the right hand over long bass notes</b> in the left. Each bass note gives that part of the melody a foundation.",
             "Holding one note while the other hand moves is a key skill. The holding hand stays still and relaxed."],
      practice:["Press the bass note firmly, then leave it held while you focus on the melody."],
      watch:["Letting go of the bass note when the right hand moves."]},
  h5:{learn:["Here the left hand plays full chords on beat 1 of each bar. The chords follow the melody's harmony: C under notes that belong to C major (C E G) and G under notes that belong to G (G B D).",
             "Hearing which chord fits which melody notes is the first step towards accompanying songs by ear."],
      practice:["Play the left-hand chords alone while you hear the melody in your head."],
      watch:["Late chords. The chord and the melody note start together."]},
  h6:{learn:["A complete piece with both hands. Real pieces combine everything so far: positions, rhythm, held notes and coordination.",
             "Professionals practise in <b>sections</b>: learn a phrase, then join it to the next one."],
      practice:["Right hand, then left hand, then both, each slower at first.","Use saved parts (🔖) to loop the tricky bits."],
      watch:["Always starting from the beginning. Practise the hard bars on their own."]},
  h7:{learn:["Broken-chord accompaniments like bottom – top – middle – top (the <b>Alberti bass</b>) give a gentle, flowing background. Mozart, Haydn and Clementi loved them.",
             "The pattern keeps an even pulse in the left hand while the right hand holds the melody."],
      practice:["Keep the left-hand pattern even and soft. It's the background."],
      watch:["Accenting every left-hand note. Let only beat 1 be a little stronger."]},
  h8:{learn:["Here the roles swap: the <b>right hand holds a chord</b> while the left hand moves. Walking bass lines that step up and down under held chords are a staple of blues and jazz.",
             "One hand has to stay completely still while the other keeps moving. That's real independence."],
      practice:["Press the chord, then put all your attention on the left hand."],
      watch:["The right hand letting go when the left starts moving."]},
  p1:{learn:["<i>Lightly Row</i> is a German folk song (\"Hänschen klein\") that moves by steps and skips in C position. Its phrases follow the pattern <b>A A B A</b>: the same phrase twice, a contrasting one, then the first again.",
             "Noticing the <b>form</b>, which phrases repeat and which differ, helps you memorise pieces faster."],
      practice:["Learn the A and B phrases separately, then put them together."],
      watch:["Rushing repeated notes. Let each one sound."]},
  p2:{learn:["<i>Frère Jacques</i> is a <b>round</b> (a canon): several people sing the same melody, each starting two bars after the last. It works because every phrase harmonises with the others.",
             "The tune is four short phrases, each played twice, which makes it good for practising patterns."],
      practice:["Count the quick eighth notes in the third phrase carefully."],
      watch:["Rushing the fast part."]},
  p3:{learn:["<i>When the Saints Go Marching In</i> is a gospel song that became a New Orleans jazz standard. It starts with a <b>pickup</b> (anacrusis): notes that come before the first strong beat.",
             "The long held notes are part of its character, so count them out fully."],
      practice:["Count \"1 2 3 4\" through every long note."],
      watch:["Cutting long notes short."]},
  p4:{learn:["<i>Ode to Joy</i> is the theme of the final movement of Beethoven's Ninth Symphony (1824) and the anthem of the European Union. It moves almost entirely by step, which makes it easy to sing.",
             "Its form is <b>A A B A</b>: the B section brings contrast, then the opening returns."],
      practice:["Learn the B section on its own first. It's the part that differs."],
      watch:["The dotted rhythm at the end of each phrase."]},
  p5:{learn:["<i>Happy Birthday</i> is in 3/4 and starts with a pickup. Its melody leaps, and the octave jump on \"happy <b>birth</b>-day dear…\" is the highlight.",
             "Leaps need preparation: look ahead and move your hand before the note is due."],
      practice:["Practise the octave leap on its own a few times."],
      watch:["The rhythm of \"hap-py\": a slightly longer note, then a short one."]},
  p6:{learn:["A <b>minuet</b> is an elegant Baroque dance in 3/4. This one, by Christian Petzold and famous through Bach's <i>Notebook for Anna Magdalena</i>, is in G major, so every F is F♯.",
             "Baroque pieces are built from short <b>motifs</b> that are repeated and varied. Spotting them makes learning much easier."],
      practice:["Use the speed trainer: start slow and build up."],
      watch:["Forgetting the F♯."]},
  p7:{learn:["Accompanying a melody with chords is the core of playing songs. Each bar's chord matches the melody notes: C under C, E and G; F under F, A and C; G under G, B and D.",
             "This is the same I – IV – V harmony you met in the Chords course."],
      practice:["Play the chords alone first, saying their names."],
      watch:["Chord changes that come late."]},
  t1:{learn:["Western music names notes with seven letters, <b>A B C D E F G</b>, which then repeat. The white keys follow this alphabet from left to right, so after G comes A again, one octave higher.",
             "Why does the piano seem to start on C? Because C major, the scale with no sharps or flats, starts on C. That's why middle C and C position are where most beginners start.",
             "Some countries use other names: <i>do re mi fa sol la si</i> (solfège) in France, Italy and Spain, and in German-speaking countries and Scandinavia B is called <b>H</b>, with B meaning B♭."],
      practice:["Say each letter out loud as you play it, forwards and backwards.","Test yourself: start on any white key and name it before you play."],
      watch:["Continuing past G to \"H\". After G comes A."]},
  t2:{learn:["A <b>half step</b> (semitone) is the smallest distance in Western music: from one key to the very next, black or white. A <b>whole step</b> (tone) is two half steps.",
             "Between E–F and B–C there's no black key, so those white keys are only a half step apart. Every other pair of neighbouring white keys is a whole step apart.",
             "Playing all twelve half steps in order is the <b>chromatic scale</b>. Using only whole steps gives the dreamy <b>whole-tone scale</b>, a favourite of Debussy and of film music for dream scenes."],
      practice:["Point at each key and say \"half\" or \"whole\" before you play it."],
      watch:["Counting a whole step from E to F♯ as E–F. E to F is only a half step."]},
  t3:{learn:["An <b>interval</b> is the distance between two notes, named by counting the letter names from the lower note to the higher, both included: C–D is a <b>2nd</b>, C–E a <b>3rd</b>, C–F a <b>4th</b>, C–G a <b>5th</b>, C–A a <b>6th</b>, C–B a <b>7th</b> and C–C an <b>octave</b>.",
             "Intervals have characters you can learn to recognise by ear. Songs help: a 4th starts <i>Here Comes the Bride</i>, a 5th starts <i>Twinkle Twinkle</i> (on \"twin-kle\"), and an octave opens <i>Somewhere Over the Rainbow</i>.",
             "The exact size matters too: a <b>major 3rd</b> (4 half steps, C–E) sounds bright, a <b>minor 3rd</b> (3 half steps, C–E♭) darker. That's the difference between major and minor chords."],
      practice:["Sing the interval before you play it, then check yourself.","Play each interval slowly and listen to its colour: open, sweet, tense?"],
      watch:["Counting the steps between the notes instead of the letter names. C to E is a 3rd (C D E), not a 2nd."]},
  t4:{learn:["Every major scale follows the same recipe: <b>W W H W W W H</b> (W = whole step, H = half step). Start on any key, follow the recipe, and you've built that key's major scale.",
             "From D: D →(W) E →(W) <b>F♯</b> →(H) G →(W) A →(W) B →(W) <b>C♯</b> →(H) D. To keep the pattern, F and C must be raised, so <b>D major has two sharps</b>: F♯ and C♯.",
             "That's what a key signature is: the sharps or flats you need so a scale fits the major recipe."],
      practice:["Build a scale on paper first: write the letters, then check each step against the recipe.","Fingering for D major is the same as C major: 1 2 3, thumb under, 1 2 3 4 5."],
      watch:["Forgetting C♯ near the top of the scale."]},
  t5:{learn:["Start at C and go up a 5th each time: C → G → D → A → E → B… Each step adds <b>one sharp</b> to the key signature. Going <b>down</b> a 5th each time (C → F → B♭ → E♭…) adds one flat instead.",
             "Laid out in a circle, these keys form the <b>circle of fifths</b>, a map of all 12 major keys. Neighbouring keys share all but one note, which is why music often moves between them (modulates).",
             "Order of sharps: F♯ C♯ G♯ D♯ A♯ E♯ B♯ (\"Father Charles Goes Down And Ends Battle\"). Order of flats is the same backwards: B♭ E♭ A♭ D♭ G♭ C♭ F♭."],
      practice:["Name the new sharp as each chord arrives: G adds F♯, D adds C♯, A adds G♯."],
      watch:["Playing C instead of C♯ in A major (A C♯ E)."]},
  t6:{learn:["A triad is two <b>thirds</b> stacked up. Which kind of third sits where decides the chord's quality:",
             "<b>Major</b> = major 3rd + minor 3rd (4 + 3 half steps), e.g. C–E–G. <b>Minor</b> = minor 3rd + major 3rd (3 + 4), e.g. C–E♭–G. <b>Diminished</b> = two minor 3rds (3 + 3), e.g. B–D–F, tense and unstable. <b>Augmented</b> = two major 3rds (4 + 4), e.g. C–E–G♯, dreamy and floating.",
             "You can build any chord by counting half steps from the root: major = root + 4 + 3, minor = root + 3 + 4."],
      practice:["Count the half steps with your eyes before playing a new chord: root, +4, +3 for major.","Play major and minor versions back to back and name the mood."],
      watch:["Moving the root or the fifth. Only the third changes between major and minor."]},
  t7:{learn:["Chords are numbered with Roman numerals after the scale note they're built on. In any major key: <b>I, ii, iii, IV, V, vi, vii°</b>. Uppercase = major, lowercase = minor, ° = diminished.",
             "The three most important chords are <b>I</b> (tonic, home), <b>IV</b> (subdominant, moving away) and <b>V</b> (dominant, tension wanting to go home). Most folk, rock and pop songs are built mainly from these three.",
             "Numerals let musicians talk about songs independently of key: a \"one-four-five\" in G is G–C–D, in C it's C–F–G and in D it's D–G–A."],
      practice:["Say the numeral aloud as you play each chord: \"one, four, five, one\".","Try the same progression in C afterwards (C–F–G–C) and hear that it sounds the same, just lower."],
      watch:["Playing F instead of F♯ in the D chord. In G major every F is F♯."]},
  t8:{learn:["A <b>time signature</b> has two numbers. The top says how many beats are in a bar, and the bottom says what kind of note counts as the beat (4 = quarter, 8 = eighth).",
             "<b>Simple</b> time (2/4, 3/4, 4/4) divides each beat in two. <b>Compound</b> time (6/8, 9/8, 12/8) divides each beat in three: 6/8 has two big beats, each split into three eighths. That gives it a lilting, rocking feel, as in jigs, lullabies and barcarolles.",
             "Strong and weak beats matter: in 4/4 beat 1 is strongest and beat 3 a little strong; in 6/8 the stresses fall on 1 and 4."],
      practice:["Count \"1 2 3 4 5 6\" with a slight accent on 1 and 4, then feel just the two big beats.","Sway from side to side on each big beat."],
      watch:["Counting 6/8 like 3/4. In 6/8 the beat groups are 3 + 3 eighths, not 2 + 2 + 2."]},
  t9:{learn:["Minor keys come in three versions. The <b>natural minor</b> uses the key signature as written (A B C D E F G A). The <b>harmonic minor</b> raises the 7th (G♯), creating a <b>leading tone</b> a half step below the home note that pulls strongly back to it.",
             "The raised 7th leaves a wide gap of 3 half steps between F and G♯ (an <b>augmented second</b>), which gives the harmonic minor its exotic sound, heard in a lot of classical, flamenco and Middle Eastern music.",
             "The <b>melodic minor</b> raises both the 6th and 7th on the way up (F♯ G♯) for a smoother line, and goes back to the natural minor on the way down."],
      practice:["Play the natural minor, then the harmonic minor, and listen to how G♯ changes the ending.","Fingering: 1 2 3, thumb under, 1 2 3 4 5, with finger 4 on G♯."],
      watch:["Forgetting the G♯ on the way back down."]},
  t10:{learn:["A <b>cadence</b> is the harmonic step that ends a phrase, the musical version of punctuation.",
              "<b>Perfect (authentic) cadence, V → I</b>: the strongest ending, a full stop. With V7 → I it's even stronger. <b>Plagal cadence, IV → I</b>: softer, the \"Amen\" at the end of hymns. <b>Half cadence, ending on V</b>: sounds unfinished, like a question or comma, so the music has to continue. <b>Deceptive cadence, V → vi</b>: you expect home but land somewhere else, a surprise.",
              "Listen for cadences in every song you play: they show you where the phrases end."],
       practice:["Play each cadence and describe its feeling in one word: final, gentle, open.","Find the cadences in a song you know, like the end of each phrase of Ode to Joy."],
       watch:["Rushing the last chord. Give endings their full length."]},
};
